### Atlas Liquidity Platform — API examples (Layer 3)
#
# Install the "REST Client" VS Code extension (humao.rest-client), then click
# the "Send Request" link above each request.
#
# Requires Postgres:  docker compose up -d

@host = http://localhost:8081

### --- Paging ------------------------------------------------------------

### Defaults: page 0, 20 rows, ascending by accountId
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts
Accept: application/json

### First page of two — page.totalElements is 6, page.last is false
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?page=0&size=2
Accept: application/json

### Last page
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?page=2&size=2
Accept: application/json

### Past the end — an empty page, not an error, so "loop until empty" is safe
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?page=99&size=20
Accept: application/json

### 400 — the page-size cap. One anonymous request must not be able to ask for
#   the whole table.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?size=999999
Accept: application/json

### 400 — negative page
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?page=-1
Accept: application/json

### 400 — Spring cannot bind "abc" to an int. A 400 only because
#   GlobalExceptionHandler extends ResponseEntityExceptionHandler.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?page=abc
Accept: application/json

### --- Sorting -----------------------------------------------------------

### Largest buffer first. JPY 500,000,000 wins on raw number — a reminder that
#   sorting money across currencies is meaningless without conversion.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?sort=liquidityBuffer&direction=desc
Accept: application/json

### Sort by a non-unique column. The adapter adds accountId as a tie-break, so
#   paging through this can never repeat or skip a row.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?sort=currencyCode&direction=asc&size=2
Accept: application/json

### 400 — unknown sort field. Names the valid ones; does NOT name our columns.
#   Without the allow-list this is a 500 leaking the entity's property names.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?sort=password
Accept: application/json

### 400 — the column name is not the API name. Deliberately independent.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?sort=liquidityBufferAmount
Accept: application/json

### 400 — unknown direction
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?direction=sideways
Accept: application/json

### --- Filtering ---------------------------------------------------------

### One filter
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?currency=USD
Accept: application/json

### Lower case works — normalised at the boundary
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?currency=usd
Accept: application/json

### Three filters, ONE SQL query. In Layer 2 this fetched by currency and then
#   sifted by jurisdiction in Java.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?currency=USD&jurisdiction=US&legalEntity=ATLAS-BANK-NA
Accept: application/json

### A contradictory combination returns nothing — not the union of the parts
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?currency=USD&jurisdiction=EU
Accept: application/json

### Blank means absent, not "match the empty string"
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?currency=
Accept: application/json

### 400 — unknown jurisdiction, with an actionable message
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?jurisdiction=ATLANTIS
Accept: application/json

### Injection attempt — bound as a JDBC parameter, matches nothing. Turn on SQL
#   logging to see why this is structurally safe rather than escaped.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts?legalEntity=x' OR '1'='1
Accept: application/json

### --- Single account ----------------------------------------------------

### Fetch one
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-EU-0001
Accept: application/json

### JPY has zero minor units — the buffer has no decimal places
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-JP-0001
Accept: application/json

### 404 — RFC 7807 problem detail
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-DOES-NOT-EXIST
Accept: application/json

### --- Absolute set (PUT, no key needed) ---------------------------------

### Set a new liquidity buffer
PUT {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-US-0001/liquidity-buffer
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "amount": "31000000.00"
}

### Send it again — PUT is idempotent, identical result, no key required
PUT {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-US-0001/liquidity-buffer
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "amount": "31000000.00"
}

### Over-precise — Money rounds HALF_EVEN to the currency's minor units
PUT {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-SG-0001/liquidity-buffer
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "amount": "9000000.0050"
}

### 400 — Bean Validation, with a field-level error map
PUT {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-US-0001/liquidity-buffer
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "amount": "not-a-number"
}

### 400 — malformed JSON is the caller's fault
PUT {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-US-0001/liquidity-buffer
Content-Type: application/json

{ this is not json

### --- The error-contract fix (Layer 3 part 1) ---------------------------

### 404, NOT 500. Before Layer 3 the catch-all turned this typo into a server
#   error, so the 5xx alert fired for nothing.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/nonexistent
Accept: application/json

### 405, NOT 500 — no DELETE on the collection
DELETE {{host}}/api/v1/accounts
Accept: application/json

### =======================================================================
### LAYER 3 PART 2 — Idempotency and OpenAPI
### =======================================================================
#
# ACC-GB-0001 starts with a buffer of 15000000.00.
#
# The REST Client extension substitutes {{$guid}} with a FRESH uuid on every
# send — which is exactly wrong for testing idempotency. So the requests below
# use a fixed key on purpose. Edit it when you want a fresh attempt.

@idempotencyKey = 6f1c9e2a-0b7d-4f1e-9a3c-2d5e8b1f4a70

### Baseline — should read 15000000.00
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-GB-0001
Accept: application/json

### Adjust +5,000,000. Look for "Idempotency-Replayed: false" in the headers.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-GB-0001/liquidity-buffer-adjustments
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{idempotencyKey}}

{
  "amount": "5000000.00",
  "reason": "Intraday buffer top-up for the CLS window"
}

### THE POINT OF THIS LAYER. Identical request, same key — the retry a real
#   client sends after a timeout. Header says "Idempotency-Replayed: true", the
#   body is byte-identical, and the buffer does NOT move again.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-GB-0001/liquidity-buffer-adjustments
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{idempotencyKey}}

{
  "amount": "5000000.00",
  "reason": "Intraday buffer top-up for the CLS window"
}

### Confirm it moved exactly once — reads 20000000.00, not 25000000.00
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-GB-0001
Accept: application/json

### 422 — same key, DIFFERENT amount. Refused outright, because honouring the
#   key here would return the +5m response and leave the client believing its
#   +50m had landed.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-GB-0001/liquidity-buffer-adjustments
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{idempotencyKey}}

{
  "amount": "50000000.00"
}

### A DIFFERENT key applies the work again — correct behaviour. Two genuinely
#   separate adjustments of the same size are legitimate; deduplicating on
#   payload alone would silently swallow the second.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-GB-0001/liquidity-buffer-adjustments
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{$guid}}

{
  "amount": "1000000.00"
}

### A negative adjustment reduces the buffer
POST {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-GB-0001/liquidity-buffer-adjustments
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{$guid}}

{
  "amount": "-1000000.00"
}

### 400 — would take the buffer below zero. Refused, and the key is NOT burned:
#   the key record and the business work share one transaction, so both roll
#   back together and the client can correct its request and reuse the key.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-GB-0001/liquidity-buffer-adjustments
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{$guid}}

{
  "amount": "-99000000.00"
}

### 400 — no Idempotency-Key header at all
POST {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-GB-0001/liquidity-buffer-adjustments
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "amount": "1000000.00"
}

### 400 — malformed amount, rejected by Bean Validation before the key is claimed
POST {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-GB-0001/liquidity-buffer-adjustments
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{$guid}}

{
  "amount": "not-a-number"
}

### 404 — unknown account
POST {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-NOPE/liquidity-buffer-adjustments
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{$guid}}

{
  "amount": "1000.00"
}

### Reset to the seeded baseline. Note: no key needed, because PUT is naturally
#   idempotent. That contrast is the whole design.
PUT {{host}}/api/v1/accounts/ACC-GB-0001/liquidity-buffer
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "amount": "15000000.00"
}

### --- OpenAPI -----------------------------------------------------------

### The machine-readable contract. This is the artefact that matters: client
#   SDKs, contract tests and API gateway config can all be generated from it.
GET {{host}}/v3/api-docs
Accept: application/json

### The interactive console. Open this one in a BROWSER:
#   http://localhost:8081/swagger-ui.html
GET {{host}}/swagger-ui/index.html

### --- Operations --------------------------------------------------------

### Supply your own correlation id and watch it in the service log
GET {{host}}/api/v1/accounts
X-Correlation-Id: dinesh-manual-test-001
Accept: application/json

### Health — includes a real database check.
#   Try: docker compose stop postgres   then hit this again.
GET {{host}}/actuator/health

### Liveness (is the process alive?)
GET {{host}}/actuator/health/liveness

### Readiness (can it actually serve traffic?)
GET {{host}}/actuator/health/readiness

### Connection pool gauges
GET {{host}}/actuator/metrics/hikaricp.connections.active
